\f1\i We are going to die\
That is what we must face\
We are the lucky ones\
Most never join the race.\
Never will they perish\
Never to falter and fall\
Owning no life to cherish\
They were never born at all.\
The countless lives that might have been,\
The selves that almost came to be,\
Outnumber all the stars we've seen,\
And all the waves upon the sea.\
Among the never-born are poets we will never read,\
And minds that would have shamed our brightest yet,\
The sum of who might live outweighs, past all that thought can heed,\
The few that chance and matter ever let.\
Against these odds too steep for quantifying\
You and I living banal and trite\
Are the privileged few, there is no denying\
Winning a grand lottery to breathe air here tonight.\
What right have we to rage and grieve\
The silence where we'll be interred,\
When all it holds is that same hush\
We knew before our name was heard.\
